body:
## Task

Stop starting the Claude CLI proxy unconditionally on every OpenCode plugin init. Lazy-start it only when a `claudecli/*` model is actually selected, and skip it entirely in headless workers (which never use claudecli/* models). Keep the EADDRINUSE adopt-on-retry as defense in depth for the rare case where two interactive sessions both legitimately need the proxy and race on bind.

## Why

Every OpenCode startup on a host running >=2 OpenCode instances prints:

```
[aidevops] Claude proxy: failed to start: Failed to start server. Is port 32125 in use?
```

Reproduced 2026-04-30 with 16 concurrent OpenCode instances on the user's host (1 interactive + 15 headless pulse workers). Only one binds 32125; the other 15 race and lose. The error is cosmetic (the proxy is up; subsequent OpenCode launches would adopt it if the probe succeeded), but it appears on every startup and erodes trust in pulse-worker telemetry.

Three independent failures stack:

1. Hardcoded port 32125 (claude-proxy.mjs:44) — every instance races for it.
2. Probe-and-adopt uses 1s timeout (claude-proxy.mjs:318) — too short when the existing proxy is mid-SSE-stream. Verified: `curl --max-time 2 http://127.0.0.1:32125/v1/models` timed out against the bound port.
3. `Bun.serve` EADDRINUSE catch logs and gives up (claude-proxy.mjs:388-394) — no re-probe, even though EADDRINUSE proves a proxy exists to adopt.

But the **root architectural issue** is upstream of the race: the proxy is starting on every plugin init, even though most sessions never select a `claudecli/*` model. Pulse workers always run with `-m anthropic/claude-X` (an Anthropic API model passed via cmdline) — they NEVER need the local Claude CLI proxy. Sibling proxies already gate on credentials:

- `cursor-proxy.mjs`: only starts if `getAccounts("cursor").length > 0` (index.mjs:151-161)
- `google-proxy.mjs`: only starts if `getAccounts("google").length > 0` (index.mjs:163-177)
- `claude-proxy.mjs`: NO gate — starts always when `claude` CLI binary is installed (index.mjs:179-187)

Lazy-load fixes the systemic cause, not just the symptom. With it, only the user's interactive session starts the proxy; the 15 pulse workers don't even try, so there's nothing to race.

## How

### Files Scope

- `EDIT: .agents/plugins/opencode-aidevops/claude-proxy.mjs:44, 315-396`
- `EDIT: .agents/plugins/opencode-aidevops/index.mjs:179-187`
- `EDIT: .agents/plugins/opencode-aidevops/config-hook.mjs` (if provider registration needs to move out of `startClaudeProxy` so the model still appears in the picker without starting the server)

### Reference pattern

Three layered fixes; each is independently valuable, all three together is the systemic answer.

**(1) Skip proxy startup in headless workers (PRIMARY — biggest impact).** In `index.mjs:179-187`, before calling `startClaudeProxy`, check for headless env vars and short-circuit:

```js
const isHeadless = process.env.AIDEVOPS_HEADLESS === "1"
  || process.env.OPENCODE_HEADLESS === "1"
  || process.env.FULL_LOOP_HEADLESS === "1"
  || process.env.GITHUB_ACTIONS === "true";

if (!isHeadless) {
  try {
    const claudeProxyResult = await startClaudeProxy(client, directory);
    if (claudeProxyResult) {
      console.error(`[aidevops] Claude proxy started on port ${claudeProxyResult.port} with ${claudeProxyResult.models.length} models`);
    }
  } catch (err) {
    console.error(`[aidevops] Claude proxy failed to start: ${err.message}`);
  }
}
```

This alone eliminates the user-visible error in the canonical case (interactive + N pulse workers).

**(2) Lazy-start on first claudecli/* request (SECONDARY — addresses the genuine multi-interactive case).** Split `startClaudeProxy` into two phases:

- **Phase 1 (init-time, cheap)**: Register the `claudecli` provider entry in `opencode.json` so the models appear in the picker. Do NOT call `Bun.serve`. Move provider registration from `launchProxyServer` into a separate `registerClaudeProviderOnly()` function called from `index.mjs` config hook.
- **Phase 2 (on-demand)**: Add an `experimental.chat.messages.transform` (or similar pre-request) hook that inspects the active model. If it's `claudecli/*` and the proxy isn't started, call `ensureClaudeProxyStarted()` which does the `Bun.serve` + EADDRINUSE retry. Block the request until the proxy is up (max ~5s).

Alternative if `chat.messages.transform` doesn't expose model/providerID: gate Phase 2 on `opencode.json` being configured to use `claudecli/*` as the default model, AND on the user setting `AIDEVOPS_CLAUDE_PROXY=1`.

**(3) Adopt-on-EADDRINUSE retry (DEFENSE IN DEPTH — for two interactive sessions that both legitimately use claudecli/*).** Keep the original t3212 design as a fallback inside `ensureClaudeProxyStarted()`:

```js
async function probeExistingProxyWithRetry(maxAttempts = 5, intervalMs = 1000, timeoutMs = 1500) {
  for (let i = 0; i < maxAttempts; i++) {
    const port = await probeExistingProxy(timeoutMs);
    if (port) return port;
    if (i < maxAttempts - 1) await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, intervalMs));
  }
  return null;
}

// In ensureClaudeProxyStarted after Bun.serve throws:
} catch (err) {
  const msg = err?.message || String(err);
  const isAddrInUse = /EADDRINUSE|address already in use|Failed to start server/i.test(msg);
  if (isAddrInUse) {
    const adoptedPort = await probeExistingProxyWithRetry();
    if (adoptedPort) {
      proxyPort = adoptedPort;
      console.error(`[aidevops] Claude proxy: adopted existing server on port ${adoptedPort} (after bind retry)`);
      return { port: adoptedPort, models: getClaudeProxyModels() };
    }
  }
  console.error(`[aidevops] Claude proxy: failed to start: ${msg}`);
}
```

Make the probe timeout configurable via `CLAUDE_PROXY_PROBE_TIMEOUT_MS` env (default 1500ms).

### Verification

```bash
# Lint
node --check .agents/plugins/opencode-aidevops/claude-proxy.mjs
node --check .agents/plugins/opencode-aidevops/index.mjs

# Headless skip — pulse workers shouldn't start the proxy
AIDEVOPS_HEADLESS=1 opencode run --print-logs --log-level WARN "test" 2>&1 | grep -c "Claude proxy" 
# Expected: 0 (no proxy startup in headless mode)

# Lazy start in interactive — proxy doesn't start until claudecli/* is picked
opencode  # start interactive, pick anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-6 model, send a message
# Expected: no "Claude proxy started on port 32125" log
opencode  # start interactive, pick claudecli/claude-opus-4-6, send a message
# Expected: "Claude proxy started on port 32125" log just before the first claudecli request

# Adopt-on-EADDRINUSE — second interactive session adopts the first one's proxy
# Run two interactive OpenCodes, both pick claudecli/* models
# Expected: second one prints "adopted existing server on port 32125" (no scary error)

# Negative test (genuine port poison)
python3 -m http.server 32125 &
sleep 1
opencode  # interactive, select claudecli/*
# Expected: original failure message after 5 retries (we don't hide real errors)
```

## Acceptance

- Headless workers (`AIDEVOPS_HEADLESS=1` or similar) NEVER start the Claude CLI proxy and NEVER print the "Failed to start server" error.
- Interactive sessions that don't select `claudecli/*` don't start the proxy either (no startup cost when the user is on Anthropic API models).
- The first `claudecli/*` request in an interactive session triggers proxy start (with at most ~5s startup latency on cold start).
- A second interactive session that legitimately uses claudecli/* adopts the first one's proxy and prints `adopted existing server on port 32125 (after bind retry)`.
- Genuine port-poison cases (non-claude-proxy listener on 32125) still produce the original error after retries — we don't hide real failures.
- Probe timeout configurable via `CLAUDE_PROXY_PROBE_TIMEOUT_MS` (default 1500ms).
- `node --check` passes on all modified files.

## Session Origin

Filed during interactive session — user reported seeing the error on every OpenCode startup. Reproduced live: port 32125 bound by a pulse worker in a managed private repo, `curl --max-time 2` timed out, 16 concurrent OpenCode instances on host (1 interactive + 15 headless pulse workers). User correctly identified that lazy-load is the architectural fix, not the EADDRINUSE retry alone.

